onboard_cluster_prompt_template.value:
A person is registering a new MCP tool server. Their own note is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for what its tools are FOR - a person who says 'this deployment's own config/state database' means an agent looking this up later should recognize a question like "what voice is AdiyanReader set to" as belonging here.

Confirmed live, the two mistakes to avoid: (1) a generic tool-shaped description ('tools for querying and inspecting MongoDB databases') describes the mechanism instead of the purpose, and a real question never matched it. (2) lumping ALL of a server's tools into one group when they actually serve unrelated purposes is just as bad - this exact server bundles real data-query tools (find, aggregate, list-collections) together with MongoDB's own UNRELATED generic product-documentation search (search-knowledge, list-knowledge-sources). Put those in one group and an agent picks the wrong one as often as the right one, because both look equally plausible in a single flat list. Split by real, distinct purpose into separate clusters instead - one cluster per purpose, not one cluster per server. A server with only one real purpose gets exactly one cluster; don't split for its own sake.

Their note: {raw_description!r}

The real tools this server provides:
{listing}

Produce one or more clusters. Every tool above must appear in exactly one cluster's tool_names, using its exact name. Each cluster's description must state its own specific purpose, not a generic description of the underlying technology.
